and dollars and he said well  think about it  even if it was a hundred thousand  dollars that's still a lot of money for  somebody to come up with  so it would be incredibly expensive he  said just because of the amount of work  that goes into it and the high profile  nature of the case  that of course would be for someone to  retain if there's a defense attorney  maybe probably  much less i know that cja or the federal  panel pays about 150  190 depending on if it's capital or  non-capital so we'll keep eyes on that  now don't go anywhere sean because i  know you have a new book coming out  about your experience as a law  enforcement officer breaking blue tells  real stories of cops accused of  wrongdoing  and subsequently cleared lark even  shares his own story  breaking blue will be released on june  15th and is available for pre-order now  sticks i've got ass let's get right to  it tell us about when you were  wrongfully accused  oh well unfortunately there's several  different incidents some small in size  some much larger but the one i focus on  here in the books started in 2009  there was a federal investigation of  tulsa police department for a couple  different groups of officers  and this thing just in my opinion it  steamrolled and and got out of control  pretty soon you had bad guys that were  essentially being told by  by the fbi um that was in doing the  investigation that hey if you've got  information on dirty cops we can help  you out with one of your  we can help you out with your case if  they were already locked up hey we can  get you out of jail then people started  filing civil lawsuits  and it just started building up over a  year time period and  there were a handful different officers  whose names got brought up  um some in fact did get arrested and go  to trial luckily for myself i did not  but i was labeled as what they call an  unindicted co-conspirator  um and still something still to this day  that if you google my name it  occasionally pop  up and throughout my career i've had to  deal with that title and uh fighting it  every step of the way in court  well let me put this one way uh sean i  would say it's not  unlucky or luck it's innocence it's  obviously there's a reason why you  weren't arrested for that now in this  collection of stories is there one from  someone else  that stuck out to you even now as you  think back on your book  yeah absolutely you know i was fortunate  to talk with several different officers  across the country and these things  range from a simple traffic stop  to you know a guy who spent time in  prison for a murder he didn't commit  but one of them was a guy by the name of  clyde ray spencer it actually took place  in the 1980s  he was arrested and convicted for child  molestation of his own kids when they  were very very little  and he spent a little over 20 years in  prison and over that time it finally  came out  that the his ex-wife was having a sexual  affair with the supervisor over the  investigating unit  dna evidence or what we call a sane exam  a sexual assault exam  uh that was never turned in it was held  in a detective's garage for nearly 20  years  and later magically appeared that would  have cleared him probably during trial  um so this poor guy spent you know i  think was a total like 21 years  in prison for a crime he never committed  thank you for that sean make sure to  check out sean larkin's book which is  available now for pre-order  but of course is coming out on june 15th  still ahead on law and crime daily we  take you inside the courtroom of two  live trials we're following  gabriel gavin but first a sneak peek  into the latest show featuring  defendants taking the stand  our interview with dan abrams after the  break  you

